 Open sessions are not held across view rendering (`spring.jpa.open-in-view` is disabled), so associations that a view needs are fetched eagerly at the mapping level.
 
+### Visit Cancellation
+
+Cancelling an upcoming visit withdraws it from the pet's record and leaves no stored trace. `Visit` gains no lifecycle state, so the [State Machine](#state-machine) section stays empty. Per [ADR: Cancellation removes the visit row rather than marking it cancelled](adr/2026-08-06-cancellation-removes-the-visit-row.md), the visit is removed from its pet's visit association and deleted by JPA orphan removal. Implements `REQ-VIS-003`.
+
+**Route.** The cancel action is a POST on an action segment under the owner-scoped visit path `VisitController` already owns; the mapping lives in the controller source. It is a POST and not a GET because it changes stored data and no caller is authenticated ([Security Context](#security-context)) — a prefetch of a GET would cancel visits. It is not a DELETE because the pages carry no client-side layer, and an HTML form issues GET or POST only.
+
+**The upcoming boundary is judged twice.** The owner detail page judges it at render time to decide whether to offer the action. The cancel handler judges it again against the visit's stored date when the submission arrives, and that second judgment is the enforcement point ([REQ-VIS-003](prd.md#req-vis-003), edge case 5). The request carries the visit's identifier and no date: nothing the caller supplies decides whether a visit is upcoming. Booking and cancellation resolve the boundary through one definition on `Visit`, because two definitions are free to drift ([ADR: Visits are cancelled, not amended or deleted](adr/2026-08-06-non-goal-visit-amendment.md)).
+
+**Refusals.** A visit that is not upcoming, and a visit identifier that names no visit under the pet and owner in the path, are both refused with the visit left in place. Each refusal redirects to the owner detail page carrying the `error` flash attribute; a completed cancellation carries `message`. Both attributes are the convention `ownerDetails.html` already renders. A missing owner, or a pet not held by that owner, continues to surface through the controller's existing not-found path rather than as a flash message.
+
+**Write path.** The visit is located by walking owner → pet → visit from the owner loaded for the request, never by a lookup keyed on a visit identifier alone. The ownership refusal is therefore structural: a visit under another owner's pet is not found. The removal reaches the database through `OwnerRepository.save` on that same owner, keeping `Owner` the sole write path for the graph ([Contracts](#contracts)). Orphan removal makes that save authoritative over each pet's visit set, so every write path must save an owner loaded whole through `OwnerRepository` ([ADR: Cancellation removes the visit row](adr/2026-08-06-cancellation-removes-the-visit-row.md)). For the same reason the blank `Visit` that backs the booking form is left off the pet's record: a handler that saves the loaded owner would otherwise cascade-persist it. `REQ-VIS-002`'s test suite guards that.
+
+**Rendering.** The visits table on the owner detail page offers the action beside an upcoming visit only. A visit dated today or earlier renders an empty action cell — no disabled control and no explanatory text, neither of which `REQ-VIS-003` asks for. The action's label resolves from the message bundles, one entry per bundle, under `REQ-LANG-002`; `I18nPropertiesSyncTest` fails the build on a hard-coded label or a bundle missing the key.
+
 ## Dependency Policy
 
 Minimize external dependencies. Every dependency is an attack surface and a maintenance burden.
